  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Function
    
    Tide Custom is a festival of conspicuous expenditure held in Aurora Stack's highest galleries during periods of clear aurora-light, binding economic display to political legitimacy. Candidates for Luminary Council seats commission ice-sculptors to carve family crests into spires; merchants unfurl bolts of aurora-charged silk glowing in wavelengths unavailable to the poor; the ambitious gift fire-cores — obtained from Nightshelf trade routes or, less officially, stolen from them — to crowds trained to measure a leader's merit by what they will burn. The summary designation is precise: the rule *demands* conspicuous expenditure as proof of fitness to govern.
    
    Access is tiered. Eight aurora fragments to display a booth. Three to stand on the mid-terrace. Free to look up from the bottom. The architecture of the fee structure ensures that poverty is visible and ambition quantifiable. It was meant to be meritocratic. It has become a tax on hope.
    
    ## Operational Provisions
    
    The festival runs only under clear aurora. Clouded skies cancel everything — leaving half-built displays and merchants with unmovable inventory — though *diminishing* aurora does not technically constitute clouded skies, a loophole the original provisions never anticipated and no subsequent authority has closed.
    
    Geological interruption ends the festival without dishonor, a provision invoked during the ice-fracture incident documented in the Promenade Ward testimonies, where a Nightshelf artifact — likely connected to the ceremonial seal Dagger☽ Vaesacsorius~ — had been corrupting the substrate for weeks before the crack surfaced. Three witnesses filed three incompatible accounts. None were cross-examined.
    
    The Organizer-of-Record may postpone the festival when no Luminary authority exists to guarantee fire-core allocation. Following Luminary Meridian-of-the-First-Light's death and the disputed succession between Claimant Prism-Ascending and Claimant Depth-Keeper, this mechanism severed the festival from its political function entirely — preserving crystals the colony could not afford to burn while denying claimants the public demonstration investiture requires.
    
    ## Exploitation and Destruction
    
    The Icebound Exchange, Aurora Stack's dominant mercantile faction, profits enormously from Tide Custom. The Nightshelf watches the fire-drain and says nothing, knowing silence is cheaper than war.
    
    When the Orca Incursion came, the galleries became the first target. Raiders burned the light-wells and left the sculptures standing — a message about the fragility of things built to shine.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771595337270_0", anchor: "The Nightshelf watches the fire-drain and says nothing, knowing silence is cheaper than war.", text: "They did not say nothing. The second secret renegotiation of the Flipper Accord contains a provision — struck before the public signing — that would have capped Aurora Stack's ceremonial fire-core expenditure at fifteen percent of seasonal allocation. The Nightshelf named the Tide Custom by title. Aurora's trade council rejected the cap. What followed was not silence. It was the quiet of someone who has already said the thing and been told it does not matter.", type: :correction, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771595337270_1", anchor: "Raiders burned the light-wells and left the sculptures standing", text: "They knew the difference. Light-wells heated the galleries. Sculptures did nothing. The orcas dismantled what sustained the colony and left what decorated it — intact, glittering, useless. That is not raiding. That is argument. I find I cannot write about orca operational intelligence without a sympathy that would disqualify me from polite scholarship. So be it. The discomfort belongs in the record.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771595337270_2", anchor: "Three witnesses filed three incompatible accounts. None were cross-examined.", text: "Two of the three survive in ice-memory. They do not merely disagree — they describe different rooms. One places the fracture origin at substrate level; the other insists propagation from above. The third, filed by an Exchange member, I have never been able to locate.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
